NAME
       umount - detach a mounted file system

SYNOPSIS
       umount [-l] [-f] [-R] [-v] DIRECTORY | DEVICE
       umount -a [-t TYPE]

DESCRIPTION
       umount detaches a file system from the directory tree. Name either the
       mount point (umount /mnt/data) or the device (umount /dev/vdb1).
       After umount the mount point is an ordinary empty directory again, and
       the data on the device is complete and safe to remove.

       umount refuses with "target is busy" while any process still uses the
       file system: has a file open on it, or has its current directory
       there. The usual cause is your own shell: cd somewhere else first. To
       find the others, use lsof DIRECTORY or fuser -vm DIRECTORY.

       umount only detaches until the next boot. If the file system is listed
       in /etc/fstab it is mounted again at boot; remove or comment out its
       line to stop that.

OPTIONS
       -l, --lazy
              Detach now and clean up once nothing uses it any more.

       -f, --force
              Force the unmount (for an NFS server that cannot be reached).

       -R, --recursive
              Also unmount everything mounted below the directory.

       -a, --all
              Unmount every file system listed in /etc/mtab (except /proc and
              some others). Rarely wanted.

       -t TYPE
              With -a: only file systems of this type, such as nfs4.

       -r, --read-only
              If unmounting fails, try to make it read-only instead.

       -v, --verbose
              Say what is being done.

EXAMPLES
       Unmount a disk mounted on /mnt/data:

           umount /mnt/data

       "target is busy": leave the directory, then try again:

           cd /
           umount /mnt/data

       Find out who still uses it:

           fuser -vm /mnt/data

       Test an /etc/fstab change without a reboot:

           umount /mnt/data
           mount -a
           findmnt /mnt/data

NOTES
       The command is umount, without an n.

SEE ALSO
       mount(8), fstab(5), findmnt(8), lsof(8), fuser(1)
